    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law and the real property law, in relation to authorizing the use of class tax rates in certain assessing units",
    "basePrintNo": "S7653",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7653-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1902, 1903, 425, 511, 581, 720 & 1111, add Art 19-A §§1911 - 1918, 1617, RPT L; amd §339-y, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7653\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law and the real property law,\nin relation to authorizing the use of class tax rates in certain\nassessing units\n\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to simplify the system for allocating\ntaxes between the homestead and non-homestead classes in certain\nassessing units.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Real Property Tax Law (\"RPTL\") §\n1902 by adding a new subdivision 5 to provide that no new Approved\nAssessing Units (\"AAU\") will be certified under RPTL Article 19 after\nDecember 31, 2010.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend RPTL § 1903 by adding a new\nsubdivision 11 to permit existing AAUs to opt into the new RPTL\nArticle 19-A (created by section 3 of the bill), rather than\ncontinuing to operate under RPTL Article 19.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would create a new Article 19-A in the RPTL to\ngovern the use of class tax rates by assessing units that conduct\nrevaluations on and after January 1, 2011. Like RPTL Article 19, the\nnew RPTL Article 19-A would authorize distinctions between the\n\"homestead\" (mostly residential) and \"non-homestead\" (mostly\ncommercial) classes for purposes of real property taxation, but it\nwould be considerably simpler to administer and understand. The new\nRPTL Article 19-A contains the following provisions:\n\n* RPTL § 1911 establishes definitions for RPTL Article 19-A, including\ndefinitions of \"homestead class\" and \"non-homestead class,\" which\nwould be the same as they are under RPTL Article 19, except that the\nhomestead class would also include co-operative apartments and could\nbe further expanded at local option to include either (1) apartment\nbuildings or (2) business property within a locally designated area,\nor both.\n\n* RPTL § 1912 provides that any assessing unit that is doing a\nrevaluation at 100% of value may apply to the State Board of Real\nProperty Services (\"SBRPS\") for certification of its revaluation as\n\"homestead-compliant.\" Upon receiving such certification, the\ngoverning body of the assessing unit may direct its assessor to\nclassify each parcel of property as either homestead or non-homestead,\nas appropriate.\n\n* RPTL § 1913 authorizes the governing body of such\nhomestead-compliant assessing unit to impose its taxes using a\ndifferent tax rate for each class of property. School districts and\nvillages which use the assessing roll for the levy of their taxes may\nopt to impose such class tax rates as well. Under RPTL § 1913, the\nclass tax rates would be set at the discretion of the governing body,\nsubject to the constraints that (1) the non-homestead class tax rate\ncould be no more than 25% higher than the homestead class tax rate,\nsubject to a 10 to 20 year phase-in of that limitation if the\n\"baseline differential\" is greater than 25%, and (2) in no case could\nthe homestead class tax rate be higher than the non-homestead class\ntax rate.\n\n\n* RPTL § 1914, requires an assessing unit to complete a\nhomestead-compliant revaluation at least once every 4 years, in order\nto remain in the program established by RPTL Article 19-A. Under RPTL\n\n§ 1914, if such a revaluation is not undertaken, the assessing unit\nand the school districts and villages associated with it would lose\ntheir ability to establish class tax rates.\n\n* RPTL § 1915 provides that the governing body of a homestead\nassessing unit may adopt a resolution terminating its status as a\nhomestead assessing unit, the effect of which would be to make\ninapplicable to such assessing unit and the municipal corporations\nwhich levy taxes upon its assessment roll the provisions of RPTL\nArticle 19-A.\n\n* RPTL § 1916 provides that a copy of any local law or resolution\nadopted pursuant to RPTL Article 19-A must be filed with the SBRPS\nwithin 30 days after such adoption.\n\n* RPTL § 1917 provides that all property in a homestead assessing unit\nmust be assessed as required by RPTL § 305(2).\n\n* RPTL § 1918 provides that an assessing unit that has been certified\nas an AAU under RPTL Article 19 would have the option to move into the\nnew RPTL Article 19-A program, without having to complete a new\nrevaluation as a condition for entry. If an assessing unit does so,\nhowever, it would be required to conduct a homestead-compliant\nrevaluation no later than its fourth year under RPTL Article 19-A, and\nat least once every four years thereafter in order to remain in the\nprogram.\n\nSections 4 through 11 of the bill would make the necessary conforming\nchanges to various other provisions of the RPTL.\n\nSection 12 of the bill would make it effective January 1, 2011,\nprovided that (1) an application for certification of a revaluation as\nhomestead-compliant pursuant to RPTL § 1912 as added by this bill, may\nbe submitted and approved prior to such date, and (2) a resolution to\nbecome a homestead assessing unit pursuant to RPTL § 1912, as added by\nthis bill, may be adopted prior to such date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nRPTL Article 19 provides that an assessing unit (other than New York\nCity and Nassau County) may become an AAU by completing a revaluation\nwhich meets the standards established by the SBRPS. RPTL § 1903\nauthorizes an AAU to establish different tax rates for the homestead\nand non-homestead classes. The AAU, however, has no discretion in\nsetting class tax rates. Rather, RPTL Article 19 prescribes the use of\nan elaborately detailed formula to determine class rates.\n\nThis formula is difficult to understand; its key elements are base\nproportions, base percentages, class equalization rates, current\npercentages, current base proportions, and adjusted base proportions.\nThe basic concept underpinning the formula is twofold: (1) it\npreserves the de facto class tax shares that existed prior to the\nrevaluation, so that taxes will not shift from one class to another as\na result of the revaluation, and (2) it requires the post-revaluation\ntax shares to be indexed to market changes, so that the disparity\nbetween the classes does not grow any wider.\n\nAdding to the complexity of Article 19 is the fact that it applies not\nonly at the AAU level but also at the \"portion\" level. Thus, a\nparallel set of complicated calculations must be performed for school\ndistricts, some villages and selected special districts which are\nwholly contained within the AAU. Yet another set of calculations must\nbe performed for certain partially contained school districts and\nvillages which qualify as \"eligible split tax districts.\"\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nA similar bill (S.7579) was introduced at the request of the SBRPS in\n2007-08.  Whereas the 2007 bill would have mandated existing AAUs to\nswitch to the new program, this bill would provide existing AAUs the\noption to switch to the new RPTL Article 19-A program.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThe hallmarks of a sound tax system are simplicity and transparency.\nRPTL.  Article 19 has neither of these attributes. It provides for the\napplication of class tax rates that are derived from an arcane\nmathematical formula. This bill would enact a new RPTL Article 19-A\nthat would maintain the homestead and non-homestead class construct.\nThe new RPTL Article 19-A, however, would provide local governments\nand their property taxpayers with a straightforward and comprehensible\nmethod for public officials to determine class tax rates. Under the\nnew RPTL Article 19-A, class tax rates would be determined in open\npublic policymaking, subject to public scrutiny.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill would require assessing units to conduct revaluations in\norder to become homestead assessing units under RPTL Article 19-A.\nThe municipal decision to seek homestead assessing unit status under\nRPTL Article 19-A, however, would be entirely voluntary. The bill,\ntherefore, does not mandate any cost on local governments. The Office\nof Real Property Services (\"ORPS\") may need to devote resources to\nsupport these additional revaluations, but such support is part of its\ncore mission and can be accomplished within its existing budget, To\nthe extent the program induces existing AAUs to leave RPTL Article 19\nand become homestead assessing units pursuant to RPTL Article 19-A,\nthis bill would produce modest savings for ORPS, which would no longer\nbe required to establish class equalization rates for such AAUs and to\nreview their adjusted base proportions.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect on January 1, 2011.\n",
